---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const contractManifest = `{
  "manifest_version": 1,
  "id": "dev.mdbase.tasknotes",
  "name": "TaskNotes",
  "homepage": "https://tasks.example",
  "redirect_uris": [
    "https://tasks.example/auth/mdbase/callback"
  ],
  "requirements": {
    "contracts": [
      { "id": "tasknotes.task", "version": 1 }
    ]
  },
  "provisions": {
    "types": [
      {
        "name": "Task",
        "document": "---\\nkind: mdbase.type\\nname: task\\nversion: 1\\nschema:\\n  dialect: json-schema-2020-12\\n  value:\\n    type: object\\nx-tasknotes:\\n  contract: tasknotes.task\\n  version: 1\\n---\\n",
        "provides": [
          { "id": "tasknotes.task", "version": 1 }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}`;

const nativeManifest = `{
  "manifest_version": 1,
  "id": "dev.example.workouts",
  "name": "Example Workouts",
  "homepage": "https://workouts.example",
  "redirect_uris": [
    "https://workouts.example/auth/mdbase/callback",
    "dev.example.workouts://auth/mdbase/callback"
  ],
  "requirements": { "contracts": [] }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Application manifest"
  description="Declare application identity, callback routes, collection compatibility, provisioning, and notification criteria in one bundled v1 file."
  active="manifest"
  headings={[
    { href: "#identity", label: "Identity and callbacks" },
    { href: "#requirements", label: "Requirements" },
    { href: "#provisioning", label: "Provisioning" },
    { href: "#native", label: "Native callbacks" },
    { href: "#changes", label: "Manifest changes" }
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The manifest is a declaration, not a remote developer registration. Bundle
    it with the application, load it through the SDK, and validate it as part of
    the build. Connect identifies the exact canonical JSON by digest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Canonical schema:
    <a href="/connect/schemas/mdbase-app.v1.json"><code>mdbase-app.v1.json</code></a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Identity and callbacks</h2>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Field</th>
        <th>Rule</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>id</code></td>
        <td>Stable lowercase reverse-domain identifier with at least three labels</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>name</code></td>
        <td>Human-readable name shown during authorization</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>homepage</code></td>
        <td>HTTPS application origin</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>redirect_uris</code></td>
        <td>HTTPS URIs on the homepage origin, plus matching native schemes when needed</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>icon</code></td>
        <td>Optional HTTPS icon on the homepage origin</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="requirements">Collection requirements</h2>
  <p>
    Requirements tell the authority which collections can satisfy the
    application. They do not grant access by themselves.
  </p>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Field</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>contracts</code></td>
        <td>Exact domain contract IDs and major versions the app understands</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>access: "contract"</code></td>
        <td>Default. Limit record access to types that provide required contracts</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>access: "full_collection"</code></td>
        <td>Request collection-wide features such as saved views or type administration</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>collection_kind: "hosted"</code></td>
        <td>Offer only provider-backed collections and enable the hosted sync handoff</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Leave collection_kind out when either authority is acceptable.</strong>
    <p>
      Route choice within a local grant is automatic. Direct versus relay is
      never a manifest requirement or a user storage choice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="provisioning">Provision a missing contract safely</h2>
  <p>
    A manifest may carry complete portable type documents. During approval, the
    authority installs only the missing definitions, reopens the collection,
    and verifies the declared contract before it creates the grant.
    Provisioning does not give the application general{" "}
    <code>create_type</code> access.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={contractManifest} language="json" label="Task contract example" />
  <p>
    A provision may only claim contracts listed in{" "}
    <code>requirements.contracts</code>. Auxiliary types installed with the
    same approved set use an empty <code>provides</code> array.
  </p>

  <h2 id="native">Native application callbacks</h2>
  <p>
    Native shells open authorization in the system browser and may list a
    private-use scheme that exactly matches, or extends, the application ID.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={nativeManifest} language="json" label="Native callback example" />
  <p>
    Pass a <code>navigate</code> adapter to <code>MdbaseConnect</code>, then
    call <code>completeAuthorization(callbackUrl)</code> when the deep link
    returns to the application.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changing the manifest</h2>
  <p>
    Any canonical manifest change creates a new application identity digest. It
    cannot silently broaden an existing grant. The existing installation stays
    bound to its approved declaration until the user completes authorization
    for the changed one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is especially important for new contracts, notification criteria, and
    changed callback routes. Handle{" "}
    <code>notification_reauthorization_required</code> by starting
    authorization again.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
